course: dictionary
lesson: 2360
title: Words from the farm
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cud is food that some animals bring back up from their stomach so they can chew it again. «A cow chews the cud: it swallows grass, later brings it back up and chews it again to break it down.» So cud is the grass itself, on its way back up to be chewed.
explain: «To chew the cud» is also a phrase. «A cow lying in a field seems to chew and chew with nothing new going in.» The cow is not eating anything new. It is chewing grass it swallowed earlier.
explain: Read the words around cud to check its meaning. Ask yourself: what is being chewed, and why? If you swap in your meaning and the sentence still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
fact: Cud is an uncountable noun, so you say some cud, not a cud.
fact: Animals that chew the cud are called ruminants. Cows, sheep, goats and deer are all ruminants.
fact: «A cow has one stomach with four parts.» Microbes in the first part break down the grass.
fact: Figuratively, «to chew the cud» means to think about something slowly and carefully.
exercise: «A cow chews the cud: it swallows grass, later brings it back up and chews it again.» What is cud? | The sound a cow makes in the field | *Food brought back up from the stomach to chew again | A kind of hay stored in a barn | A hard shell on a cow's ear
exercise: «He sat by the window and chewed the cud for an hour before he decided.» What does chewed the cud mean here? | Chewed gum very loudly | *Thought about it slowly and carefully | Fell asleep in his chair | Ate a snack by the window
exercise: Which of these animals is a ruminant that chews the cud, like a cow? | Dolphin | Lion | *Goat | Eagle
test: «A cow lies in the field and seems to chew with nothing new going in.» Why? | *It is chewing grass it swallowed earlier, which is cud | It is chewing its own tail | Lying down makes it sleepy | It is trying to make milk faster
test: A cow's stomach has how many parts? | Ten | Seven | *Four | Two
exam: Mia says, «Sam chewed the cud on the problem all afternoon.» What does Mia mean? | Sam was bored and went to sleep | *Sam thought about the problem slowly and carefully | Sam was a cow in a field | Sam ate grass all afternoon
